// resolution this chart does not have. A 15-minute bar that both touches the
// entry edge and breaches the far edge contains production's fill and
// production's invalidation at once, and OHLC cannot order the minutes
// between them.
//
// THE TWO MODES DIFFER IN EXACTLY ONE DECISION, and it is not the one a
// reader would guess. Neither can decline the bar above: TradingView matches
// a resting order against a bar BEFORE the script is evaluated at that bar's
// close, so if such a bar delivers the entry the fill has already happened.
// Both modes therefore only refuse to START, and both count the fills whose
// own bar broke the block.
//
// What they do differ on is the ARM BAR. If the bar that armed a setup had
// already reached the entry edge, production — on 1-minute candles — may well
// have filled inside it; 28 of 46 recorded fills (61%) did. This build cannot
// trade a bar that has closed, so its order waits for a later bar to revisit
// the edge: same price, different hour, therefore a different session, market
// state and possibly matrix cell. STRICT will not call that production's
// trade and declines. PRACTICAL takes it, flags it emulator-dependent, and
// labels every headline APPROXIMATE. Neither is called parity.
//
// Python is the sole source of truth. Where this strategy and the production
